# API conventions

- Keep endpoint paths in one frontend API layer; UI components never construct URLs.
- Use kebab-case URLs, snake_case JSON fields, and camelCase TypeScript methods.
- Use POST to create, PATCH to update, and DELETE for delete/archive according to the documented contract.
- Successful create/update responses return the current API Resource.
- Successful deletion returns explicit HTTP 204.
- Validation failures use HTTP 422 with `{ errors: { field: string[] } }`.
- Business conflicts use a stable machine-readable code and a user-safe message.
- Do not silently unwrap different response shapes in different endpoints.
- Paginated endpoints use one consistent data/meta structure.
- Authenticate web requests using secure HttpOnly cookies when the selected auth approach supports it.
- Never expose internal exception details or stack traces to clients.
- Keep authorization enforced on the backend even when the frontend hides unavailable controls.
- Update API types and tests whenever the public contract changes.
- Introduce OpenAPI/code generation only when the initial contract is stable enough to justify it.
